# ============================================================
# THE SIGN INDEX — Kaalchakra
# ============================================================
#
# Every sign Eva can decipher. Edit this in TextEdit; the game reads it.
#
# The chain is the chapter: LEARN a sign fresh in 1900 BCE, MATCH it eroded
# in 2026, CONFIRM it in WARDEN's scan in 2087. One sign, three mornings.
# That is the whole mechanic, so each sign needs all three lines written.
#
#   ::        starts a sign; the word after it is its id
#   name:     what it means, shown once deciphered
#   gloss:    what it looks like, for an artist to draw
#   learn:    Eva's line the moment she understands it (1900 BCE)
#   match:    her line finding it eroded (2026)
#   confirm:  her line verifying it (2087)
#   hint:     what Sakshi says if the player asks for help
#   header:   position in the chamber's header phrase, if it is in it
#
# A bare indented line continues the one above, same as the dialogue files.
# ------------------------------------------------------------

:: what
name: WHAT / THAT WHICH
gloss: a single upright stroke with a hook at the head — the opener
header: 1
learn: It's not a word. It's a bracket — it says "everything after me is one
    thing". Ma called it the opener and nobody believed her.
match: Same hook. Half the stroke is gone to salt, but the hook survived.
confirm: WARDEN has it in nine hundred inscriptions and always in first
    position. Always. That's not a word, that's punctuation.
hint: Sakshi: "Count how often it comes first. If a sign is always first, it
    isn't telling you anything — it's telling you how to read."

:: water
name: WATER
gloss: three stacked waves under a jar outline
header: 2
learn: Vasudha draws it in spilled water on the bench, which is either a joke
    or the most efficient teaching I've ever had.
match: On the basin wall, under the waterline. Of course it's under the
    waterline.
confirm: The scan resolves the stroke order — jar first, then the waves. The
    container before the thing contained. They thought about water the way an
    engineer does.
hint: Sakshi: "Look for it where water was kept, not where water ran. These
    people labelled storage, not rivers."

:: not
name: MUST NOT / FORBIDDEN
gloss: two crossed strokes over a low bar
header: 3
learn: A prohibition. Vasudha says it on the reservoir wall every day of her
    life and has never once had to explain it.
match: Cut deeper than the signs around it. Someone wanted this one to outlast
    the others.
confirm: Sixty years underwater and it's still the sharpest sign on the wall.
    They cut the warnings deepest.
hint: Sakshi: "The deepest-cut sign in any inscription is almost always a
    warning. People carve fear harder than they carve names."

:: take
name: TAKE / CARRY AWAY
gloss: an open hand under a tilted vessel
header: 4
learn: Not "steal". Closer to "carry off" — what a river does, or a trader.
    The same sign for both, which tells you how they felt about both.
match: Barely there. A hand with no fingers left.
confirm: WARDEN cross-references it against the dock tablets. Cargo
    manifests. It's an inventory word.
hint: Sakshi: "Try it against the dock inventories, not the ritual texts.
    It's a logistics word — I always said this script was a warehouse, not a
    temple."

:: house
name: KNOWLEDGE-HOUSE
gloss: a roofed rectangle divided into four cells
learn: Four rooms under one roof. Vasudha says it like a place you can walk
    into, because it is.
match: On the lintel. Of course it's on the lintel — you label the door.
confirm: The Archive's own founding record uses this sign as its name. WARDEN
    has been called this for sixty years and never knew why.
hint: Sakshi: "If the script is an index, something has to be indexed. Find
    the sign for the building that holds the thing."

:: measure
name: MEASURE / TALLY
gloss: five short bars and a longer closing stroke
learn: A count, and a unit. These people measured everything and wrote the
    unit down every single time, which is more than most of my colleagues do.
match: Repeated eleven times along the drain. Someone was counting capacity.
confirm: It resolves to a volume — and the volume matches Vasudha's
    flood-timing tables exactly.
hint: Sakshi: "Where the same sign repeats in a run, you're looking at
    numbers. Nobody writes a poem eleven times in a row."

:: grain
name: GRAIN STORE
gloss: a seed-shape inside a square
learn: Seed, in a box. There is no poetry in this script at all and I love it
    for that.
match: Three of them in the lower city, all on the same street.
confirm: The Archive holds the seed strains themselves. Still viable. Sixty
    years under water and someone kept them viable.
hint: Sakshi: "Food storage is always labelled and always near the road. Walk
    the street, not the ruins."

:: keeper
name: THE KEEPER
gloss: an eight-spoked disc above an open palm
learn: Vasudha doesn't teach me this one. She points at me and then at the
    wall, and waits.
match: The mark on the apron slab. The one that sent me down here.
confirm: WARDEN's ledger of Keepers opens with it. It has a column for
    centuries, and the column is not short.
hint: Sakshi: "You already know this one, beta. You've been carrying it."
